  "example_content": [
    {
      "text": "The grocery receipt comparisons going viral are interesting but methodologically flawed. CPI tracks a rotating basket with substitution effects (chicken\u2192turkey if chicken spikes). Personal receipts track fixed items. Both are valid perspectives \u2014 CPI measures 'cost of living at similar quality,' receipts measure 'cost of the same stuff.'",
      "platform": "reddit",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "is_influencer_framing": false
    },
    {
      "text": "I did a rigorous version of the grocery receipt comparison: same 40 items, same store, same brands, using my actual receipts from January 2020 vs January 2024. Total increase: 38%. BLS CPI food-at-home for the same period: 25%. The gap is mostly explained by above-average increases in eggs (+89%), butter (+32%), and bread (+36%).",
